<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As he puts the finishing touches to tomorr=
ow=E2=80=99s budget, John Healey may note that another bank has reported a =
jump in earnings.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">HSBC is giving $2.7bn (=C2=A32bn) to share=
holders after a 32% rise in profits, which will assure the Treasury that th=
e UK=E2=80=99s top banks can shoulder a tax increase in tomorrow=E2=80=99s =
budget.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The London-headquartered bank reported thi=
s morning that profits grew almost a third to $9.6bn in the July to Septemb=
er quarter =E2=80=93 slightly below consensus estimates =E2=80=93 helped by=
 higher interest rates, lower loan losses and smaller legal provisions comp=
ared with last year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It meant the bank could give $2.7bn to sha=
reholders. That included another $1bn share buyback, as well as an interim =
dividend of $0.1 a share, which the bank said would mean distributing a fur=
ther $1.71bn to its investors.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Net interest income =E2=80=93 the differen=
ce between what banks charge for loans versus what they pay out for savers=
=E2=80=99 deposits =E2=80=93 rose to $9.3bn from $8.8bn year on year. Howev=
er, that was apparently lifted by its hedging strategy and a growth in depo=
sits in Hong Kong, while UK deposits stayed flat.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">HSBC is following Barclays, which last wee=
k reported a 12% jump in profits.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">HSBC=E2=80=99s jump in profits is promptin=
g fresh calls for new bank taxes in the budget.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As the Guardian <a href=3D"https://ablink.=
editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSn=
eHEAuG0RqCca7fIwka-i3erzpEUdW3oO2KuYq44N90qq51b7wxABED9MCV-xvqIrbGYine810iA=
UY4s5nopGsT_nRkAhgODblGGdK18gWka2OObQrJpGDPhZD1coGypEZbJhRRag31obPiksCAV3rl=
4R8xkI3rFlTo2g9NCzCgdk9l546CO_Cnf05iufTQ1NIEZD-tD-hBjXQPKU0m65eO4Pa-ebkX9Ap=
HxB3DnzY5Ez1sljapa09BPIdZnL-Jsy8Zas/4v8/RR4cpv5zTEiKOGet5O3mEg/h30/h001.5v4=
yyXqz-pnEbkQXPB493dXwMa-1ndDaxjd569bmRkA" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">reporte=
d earlier this month</a>, Healey has decided to tap the financial sector fo=
r additional revenue. One way would be to reverse cuts to the bank surcharg=
e made by the former chancellor Jeremy Hunt =E2=80=93 which would raise upw=
ards of =C2=A32bn a year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Abhijay Sood, an economist at the New Econ=
omics Foundation, said Healey should go further.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">He said: =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s not surpris=
ing that HSBC have made such significant profits in the last quarter, but i=
t will be difficult news to swallow for households struggling with rising m=
ortgage costs. Like other big banks, its profitability has depended on keep=
ing interest rates on savings accounts low, and a =E2=80=98stealth subsidy=
=E2=80=99 from taxpayers via the Bank of England due to quantitative easing=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CWhile the rumours of a bank tax c=
hange in the budget are welcome, it is unlikely to go far enough. While ban=
ks are making over a billion pounds a week of profit, NEF analysis suggests=
 that the restoration of the bank surcharge to 8% would raise just =C2=A31.=
8bn a year from them. The chancellor should go further by clawing back the =
stealth subsidy, and use this to fund inflation-busting investment and keep=
 the essentials of life affordable for ordinary people.=E2=80=9D</p>
